## Why This Is Asked
Real-world ETL and streaming services need to evolve without redeploys. A runtime registry and composable pipeline let teams add or replace enrichment steps on the fly, reducing downtime.

## Key Concepts
- Runtime registries and factories
- Transformer pattern with async support
- Pipeline composition and backpressure
- Schema compatibility and versioning trade-offs

## Code Example
```typescript
interface Transformer<I,O> {
  transform(input:I): Promise<O>;
}
type Factory<T> = () => T;
class Registry {
  private map = new Map<string, Factory<any>>();
  register<T>(name:string, f: Factory<T>): void { this.map.set(name, f); }
  create<T>(name:string): T | null { const f = this.map.get(name); return f ? f() as T : null; }
}
class Pipeline<I> {
  constructor(private steps: Transformer<any,any>[]) {}
  async run(input:I): Promise<any> {
    let acc: any = input;
    for (const s of this.steps) {
      acc = await s.transform(acc);
    }
    return acc;
  }
}
// Usage example
const registry = new Registry();
registry.register('geo', () => ({ transform: async (i:any)=> ({ ...i, geo: 'US' }) }));
registry.register('currency', () => ({ transform: async (i:any)=> ({ ...i, currency: 'USD' }) }));
const steps = [registry.create<any>('geo')!, registry.create<any>('currency')!];
const pipeline = new Pipeline<any>({} as any); // placeholder to illustrate
pipeline['steps'] = steps;
pipeline.run({ id: 1, value: 100 }).then(console.log);
``` 

